# Exact affine-lattice regression near the hexagonal density

This is a finite, exact obstruction to a density-only proof of the Target A
statement. It is not a global counterexample to Erdős Problem #99.

For \(0<\varepsilon<1\), let \(u,v\) be vectors with

\[
|u|=1,\qquad |v|=1+\varepsilon,\qquad u\cdot v=\frac{1+\varepsilon}{2}.
\]

For an integer pair \((a,b)\), the squared norm in
\(\Lambda_\varepsilon=\mathbb Zu+\mathbb Zv\) is

\[
\|au+bv\|^2
=a^2+(1+\varepsilon)ab+(1+\varepsilon)^2b^2.
\]

For \(0<\varepsilon<1\), the only nonzero lattice differences of norm one are
\(\pm u\). In particular, the unit-distance graph of every finite subset of
\(\Lambda_\varepsilon\) is a union of parallel chains and is triangle-free. The
fundamental area is

\[
\det\Lambda_\varepsilon
=\frac{\sqrt3}{2}(1+\varepsilon),
\]

which tends to the hexagonal-lattice area \(\sqrt3/2\) as
\(\varepsilon\downarrow0\).

For the \(L\times L\) coordinate patch

\[
P_{L,\varepsilon}
=\{iu+jv:0\le i,j\le L-1\},
\]

the exact minimum squared distance is \(1\), the patch is triangle-free, and its
diameter is

\[
\operatorname{diam}(P_{L,\varepsilon})^2
=(L-1)^2\bigl(3+3\varepsilon+\varepsilon^2\bigr).
\]

Taking \(\varepsilon=\alpha/L\) gives a triangle-free family whose determinant
excess over the hexagonal optimum is \(O(1/L)\), and whose finite patch diameter
differs from the unstrained patch by only \(O(1)\). Thus:

- triangle-freeness does force strict loss for a fixed periodic lattice;
- the loss has no uniform positive lower bound;
- a finite patch can make the loss boundary-scale by taking
  \(\varepsilon=O(1/L)\).

This is the affine-lattice version of the distributed-strain regression in the
canonical theorem state. It rules out attempts to finish Target A using only an
asymptotic density gap or a fixed positive cost per triangle-free cell. Any
successful argument must use the exact finite diameter boundary, source-specific
capacity, or a comparable global invariant.

The exact executable check is in research/affine_lattice.py, with tests in
research/test_affine_lattice.py.
